      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Tracing the arc of Social Capital Theory, scholars like Pierre Bourdieu and James Coleman reshaped the understanding of social relationships and resources. Robert Putnam later became a central figure by underscoring citizenship, trust, and cultural norms as central elements of social capital. Nan Lin built upon this foundation by linking social networks and their associated resources with social exchange. Mark Granovetter's insights into social networks and the strength of weak ties significantly expanded the concept, while Max Cutler and Alex Inkpen's research delved into the behavioral aspects of social capital.\n\nThe contributions of David Knoke, John Peter, Stuart Lynn, Janet Lynn, Frank D'souza, Jeffrey Gove, Frank Massey, Jan Medel, Jeffrey Roth, Sandy McKee, Jeffrey Rogers, Peter Evans, James Y. L. Fu, Michael A. T. Newman, Howard Aldrich, Leonard Songer, Boel Bernaars, and others have systematically explored social capital's functional role in various societal contexts. Harold P. Nelson's extensive work on institutional networks within sports, science, and politics, as well as Katherine Tucker's study on science and technology elites, have extended our comprehension of social structures and capital within these sectors.\n\nExpanding comprehension of social",
